.github/copilot-instructions.md, .cursor/rules, etc.) — anything an AI coding
agent reads at the start of a session. Requires an MCP client already
connected to a graph-rag memory server (docker-compose.memory.yml on :8766,
or the combined default stack on :8765 with `serve-mcp --role all`), exposing
its `remember` / `recall` / `forget` tools. See ../README.md for setup.
-->

## Persistent memory (graph-rag) — REQUIRED

This project has an MCP-backed memory server. Using its tools is not optional
— it is how you keep continuity across sessions instead of re-deriving the
same context every time.

### At the start of every task

Call **`recall(query, top_k=5, kind=None, about_qualified_name=None)`** before
doing anything else. It's a semantic search, so phrase `query` as a sentence
describing the task, not a keyword. Fold anything it returns into your plan.

### While you work

Call **`remember(content, kind, about_qualified_name=None, importance=False)`**
the moment one of these happens — do not batch it, do not wait until the end
of the session to write a summary:

- `decision` — a choice made and why (architecture, library, approach).
- `correction` — the user corrected your approach; record what and why, so it
  isn't repeated.
- `finding` — something learned about the codebase that isn't obvious from
  reading it (a gotcha, a non-obvious invariant, a root cause).
- `preference` — a standing preference about how to work in this project.
- `fact` — a piece of project state that will go stale (a deadline, an owner,
  a decision pending elsewhere).

Set `importance=True` for anything that should never be pruned by decay
(`grag-mcp prune-memory`) even if it's never recalled again — e.g. a standing
constraint, not a one-off finding.

Tag a memory with `about_qualified_name=<dotted.path.to.Entity>` when it's
about one specific function/class/module — this makes it filterable later even
if the memory server runs separately from the code's own knowledge base.

### Before you end your turn

Task completion includes memory. Before your final reply on any task, review
what happened in this turn and confirm you called `remember` for every
decision, correction, finding, and preference above. Saving nothing is a
deliberate choice you should be able to justify — not the default.

### Corrections

Call **`forget(memory_id)`** immediately if a memory turns out to be wrong or
no longer applies, then `remember` the corrected version. Don't leave stale
memories for decay to eventually clean up if you already know they're wrong.

### Do not save

Information already derivable from reading the code, git history, or this file;
ephemeral in-progress task state; anything that belongs in a commit message or
PR description instead.
